{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_succ","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QBorelKernel.lean#L44-L50","doc":"**The q-Borel kernel recurrence** `t_{n+1} = q^n · tₙ` (the level-1 case\n`tₙ = q^{n−1}·t_{n−1}` of the RSZ family relation). Proof: `C(n+1,2) = C(n,1) + C(n,2) =\nn + C(n,2)` (Pascal), so `q^{C(n+1,2)} = q^n · q^{C(n,2)}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_one","kind":"theorem","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QBorelKernel.lean#L41-L42","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel_zero","kind":"theorem","line":38,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QBorelKernel.lean#L38-L39","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QBorelKernel.qBorelKernel","kind":"def","line":33,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QBorelKernel.lean#L33-L36","doc":"**The level-1 q-Borel (Jacobi-theta) kernel** `tₙ = q^{C(n,2)} = q^{n(n−1)/2}`. This is\nthe canonical kernel of the q-Borel transform `B_{q,1}` resolving an integral-slope\nsingularity. ","signatureChunk":0}]}